// A program of MORE THAN ONE MODULE is checked, and that is the whole
// assertion.
//
// A `Parser` used to build its own `Ast`, so N files were N unrelated id
// spaces and a `Checker` — which is built from ONE tree and answers
// about ids — could only ever be handed a one-module program. The driver
// now hands the tree from parser to parser and reads it back, so one
// counter names every node in the compilation.
//
// `pair/` is two modules and the second is reached only by following an
// import, which is what makes this different from the one-module root
// beside it. Before the tree was threaded this printed "2 modules
// parsed; sema needs them in one Ast" and exited 1, so a `0` here is the
// whole of the change.
//
// WHAT IT DOES NOT PIN, said rather than implied: that EVERY module
// reached the tree. A driver that kept only the last parser's tree would
// check `pair.zen` alone, and `pair.zen` alone is a clean module — so
// this test would still be green. Pinning that needs the LAST module
// parsed to be the one that cannot stand alone, and the walk parses the
// entry first; the module that depends on another is therefore always
// the one a broken driver drops. `zen build src` is the test that
// catches it, and it is not a corpus test yet.
//
// `build` is called directly rather than through `main` because
// `env.argv` is empty in the generated C — see the report — so a real
// command line cannot reach it yet.

Alloc, AllocError = std.mem
str = std.text
Debug, Job, build = zen

main = (env: Env) Res<i32, AllocError> {
    a ::= env.mem.alloc();
    run(env, a, "src/pair").try();
    Ok(0);
}

run = (env: Env, a: Alloc, root: str) Res<(), AllocError> {
    code = build(
        env,
        a,
        Job(
            root: root,
            out: "",
            emit_c: false,
            debug: Debug(repeat: 1, permute: "")
        )
    ).try();
    println("{} -> {}", root, code);
    Ok(());
}
